{
  "id": "80706",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Homogeneous and Heterogeneous Equilibria ",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "isqho58c5wn2gy6",
                "text": "Homogeneous Equilibria"
              }
            ],
            "id": "reae7407wz24wiz",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A homogeneous equilibrium is one in which all of the reactants and products are in the same phase. The most common types of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "homogeneous equilibria"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Equilibria established between substances that are all in the same phase."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3442763561",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3693271122",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " are liquid-phase solutions and those involving exclusively gaseous species; for example:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "e4tc40djl0xp6e8",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "lo68rvvb06kqafn",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "p5hxggueh9sselu",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm I}_2(aq)+\\mathrm I^-(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm I_3^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm I_3^-\\right]}{\\left[{\\mathrm I}_2\\right]\\left[\\mathrm I^-\\right]}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "stcae9vz95v8kp2",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "evo3gs5vk7z28z2",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm H}_6(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm H}_4(g)+{\\mathrm H}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; K_\\mathrm{p}=\\frac{\\left({\\mathrm P}_{{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm H}_4}\\right)\\left({\\mathrm P}_{{\\mathrm H}_2}\\right)}{\\left({\\mathrm P} _{{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm H}_6}\\right)}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "So far, we have primarily encountered homogeneous gas-phase reactions for which we can use molar concentrations or partial pressures to calculate "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "c"
              },
              {
                "text": " or "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "p"
              },
              {
                "text": " respectively. However, many equilibria include substances with different phases."
              }
            ],
            "id": "p73tp0rl3sfy3s5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "qcm92sfxopd9b75",
                "text": "Heterogeneous Equilibria"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5u8x5pe0agrev0j",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A heterogeneous equilibrium is a system in which reactants and products are found in two or more phases. The phases may be any combination of solid, liquid, or gas phases and solutions. When dealing with "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "heterogeneous equilibria"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Equilibria established between substances in two or more phases, such as a gas and a liquid."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "120443809",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2914619776",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", solids and pure liquids do not appear in equilibrium constant expressions. Let's consider an example that shows why pure solids and liquids are not included in the equilibrium expression."
              }
            ],
            "id": "cov04tcfc85kosk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When solid ammonium sulfate is heated above 235 °C, it decomposes into ammonia and vaporous sulfuric acid in a reversible reaction, as shown below:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1vkdf1qogg71bco",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4qxoh72z8c849c5",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xp3aupbqbf93fvl",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "({\\mathrm{NH}}_4)_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_4(s)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{NH}}_3(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_4(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "At temperatures above 235 °C, solid ammonium sulfate, (NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "SO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ","
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " decomposes into ammonia, NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "g"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ","
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and vaporous sulfuric acid, H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "SO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "g"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ")"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ","
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " in a reversible reaction. "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3626525267",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "o7slxxbegyd5npg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "p2td5xhqi62m7rk",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bf7f00f2d5f3b016472c35e17f50a3bd/ammonium_heated.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "500"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If (NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " is heated sufficiently, for example, to 350 °C, it decomposes as indicated in the reaction equation until equilibrium is reached. At equilibrium, there will be constant amounts of NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " and H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " gas as well as a constant amount of solid (NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Although the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "amount"
              },
              {
                "text": " of (NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") changes (decreases) from before the heating started until equilibrium is reached, its "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "concentration"
              },
              {
                "text": " does not change because its volume and mass decrease proportionally. Equilibrium constant expressions include only substances that change their concentrations, so for this reaction we have:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5aq54nyyh0jy1t5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rjojk3jkwa6knqi",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "pobjtk26w289om3",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_\\mathrm{c}=\\left[{\\mathrm{NH}}_3\\right]\\left[{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_4\\right]\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{or}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; K_\\mathrm{p}=\\left(P_{{\\mathrm{NH}}_3}\\right)\\left(P_{{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{SO}}_4}\\right)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this case, (NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is omitted from the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " expressions because it is a pure solid and its concentration does not change. In general, pure solids and pure liquids have constant concentrations, so they are not included in equilibrium constant or reaction quotient expressions. (As mentioned previously, the rigorous versions of the equilibrium constant and reaction quotient expressions use relative concentrations. The relative concentrations for pure solids and pure liquids are equal to 1, so they have no effect on these expressions.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9outyq2440npl99",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Here are some examples of heterogeneous equilibria and their equilibrium constant expressions:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "35umzj4sap4i0fj",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4c8q5huchb8eqqi",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "l87qfdrjzsuiu7a",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{PbCl}}_2(s)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}(aq)+2\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\lbrack\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{Cl}^-\\rbrack^2",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "yxaw2gktl0tfgr0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rb7cubozpi7dv3o",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{Br}}_2(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{Br}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\lbrack{\\mathrm{Br}}_2\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "asjptmj5lc84yf0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ns8ix02ookrw2cl",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{CaO}(s)+{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{CaCO}}_3(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\frac1{\\left[{\\mathrm{CO}}_2\\right]}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Note that for reactions that include only gases in their reaction quotient or equilibrium constant expressions, we can use partial pressures instead of concentrations. For example, for the reaction of carbon and sulfur to make carbon disulfide, we can use:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "7i6h56n7keo73bi",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ve3llydxchmg39c",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "sj0iaozi4z8jzxo",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm C(s)+2\\;\\mathrm S(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{CS}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\frac{\\lbrack{\\mathrm{CS}}_2\\rbrack}{\\lbrack\\mathrm S\\rbrack^2}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "or"
              }
            ],
            "id": "13sfuto6zkm7qf3",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ay1jkupr5ndb87d",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rcn0fkc9393qe97",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm C(s)+2\\;\\mathrm S(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{CS}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{p}=\\frac{\\;P_{{\\mathrm{CS}}_2}}{\\left({\\mathrm P}_{\\mathrm S}\\right)^2}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Reactions in solution may include the solvent as a reactant or product. If the solution is "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "dilute"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A qualitative term for a solution containing solute at a relatively low concentration."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1540965757",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3193086956",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " enough, the concentration of the solvent does not change much during the reaction, and we can treat the solvent as essentially a pure substance. This generally is the case when we encounter aqueous solution reactions involving water. Some examples are:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "je9h304rgyqpx36",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "sz5v9k4brfetmwq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4wf3f2jue0acpas",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{HF}(aq)+{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)+\\mathrm F^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\frac{\\left[{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\right]\\left[\\mathrm F^-\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{HF}\\right]}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "2aotcwdkezk1rdk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8yet2isipk1mlrk",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{NH}}_3(aq)+{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm{NH}_4^+(aq)+\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_\\mathrm{c}=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_4^+\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^-\\right]}{\\left[{\\mathrm{NH}}_3\\right]}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "l"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is the solvent for these solutions. Its concentration is essentially constant, so it does not appear as a term in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "c"
              },
              {
                "text": " expression even though it may also appear as a reactant or product in the reaction equation."
              }
            ],
            "id": "f7qda90l2ngqu4x",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
